{
  /* M2 note: this is the pre-M2 root tsconfig.json, renamed verbatim and
     otherwise untouched. `lib/` must keep being checked under real
     NodeNext resolution — see the comment just below for why — so when
     Next.js needed the root tsconfig.json for its own bundler-resolution
     rules (see ../tsconfig.json), this file was carved out rather than
     loosened in place. `npm run typecheck` now runs both configs as two
     separate `tsc` invocations.

     M7 note: PLAN.md puts the attack suite under a top-level "tests"
     directory, but until now this file's own "include" list (below)
     only reached down into "lib", and the root tsconfig.json only
     reaches into "app" — a suite living under "tests" would type-check
     NOTHING, a silent hole in a milestone whose whole point is rigour
     (a typo'd import, a wrong Extract-narrowing mistake, or a stale
     field name in an attack test would silently never be caught by
     "npm run typecheck", only by actually running the tests — and even
     then maybe not, if the mistake made a test vacuously pass).
     Resolved deliberately by widening THIS file's own "include" array
     (below) to also reach the "tests" tree, rather than moving the
     suite somewhere already covered: the tests tree needs the exact
     same NodeNext resolution and strictness this file already enforces
     for "lib" (relative imports use explicit ".js" extensions, same as
     every existing test file under "lib"), so widening THIS config is
     the natural fit — the root tsconfig.json (bundler resolution, DOM
     lib, JSX) is for "app" and would be the wrong tool. The existing
     "npm run typecheck" script's "tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.lib.json"
     invocation therefore now ALSO covers the attack suite, with no
     change to package.json's script itself needed — proven with
     "tsc --listFiles -p tsconfig.lib.json", grepping for the tests
     tree's own files in that output. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* Language / module target.
       NodeNext resolution mirrors how Node actually resolves ESM at runtime
       (explicit ".js" extensions on relative imports, package "exports"
       maps honoured) so `tsc --noEmit` catches resolution mistakes that a
       looser setting like "bundler" would hide until Next.js runs it. */
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    /* M3 note: TypeScript's automatic "include every ambient @types/*
       package" behavior turns out NOT to fire for this config (verified
       with `tsc --listFiles`: zero @types/node files were loaded) —
       M1 never noticed because lib/identity never referenced a Node
       global (no Buffer, no TextDecoder). M3's JWS layer needs
       `TextDecoder` (a real Node AND browser global, unlike
       `atob`/`btoa`, which are typed only via lib.dom.d.ts and are
       deliberately NOT used here for exactly that reason — see
       lib/credentials/base64url.ts's own comment). Naming "node"
       explicitly is the standard fix for that gap; it adds no
       DOM-shaped typings and loosens no strictness flag below. */
    "types": ["node"],

    /* Strictness — non-negotiable per project ground rules. */
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    /* Emit nothing — this project only ever runs through tsx/vitest/Next's
       own compiler. `npm run typecheck` exists purely to type-check. */
    "noEmit": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true
  },
  "include": ["lib/**/*.ts", "tests/**/*.ts"]
}
